# Two-dimensional light-emitting materials: preparation, properties and applications

## abstract
The past decade has witnessed tremendous research efforts devoted to two-dimensional (2D) materials and great progress made in both their fundamental studies and technique development. 2D light-emitting materials such as transition-metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) and phosphorene are receiving particular attention as a result of their intriguing electronic, optical and optoelectronic properties. The ability to tune their layer numbers, engineer their dielectric environment, form alloys, create van der Waals heterostructures, etc. provides further means to modulate their properties which have led to a number of interesting luminescence features and potential applications in lighting, imaging and sensing. As the map of 2D materials has expanded over the past few years, 2D organic and organic-inorganic hybrid materials, such as 2D polymers, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), and organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites, have also emerged with merits of low cost, chemical versatility and solution processibility. Importantly, the wide tunability of their functional properties is enabled by the rational design and controlled modification of their compositions and structures at the molecular level. Herein, we present a critical review of 2D light-emitting materials by categorizing them into three main groups, namely 2D inorganic light-emitting materials, 2D organic light-emitting materials, and 2D organic-inorganic hybrid light-emitting materials. Within each group, we will describe their preparation methods and discuss their structural and chemical properties. Significant emphasis will be placed on strategies that can tune their luminescence properties, and their potential applications in various fields. Finally, we will summarize by stating the current challenges and future opportunities in the development of 2D light-emitting materials.

The past decade has witnessed tremendous research efforts devoted to two-dimensional (2D) materials and great progress made in both their fundamental studies and technique development. 2D light-emitting materials such as transition-metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) and phosphorene are receiving particular attention as a result of their intriguing electronic, optical and optoelectronic properties. The ability to tune their layer numbers, engineer their dielectric environment, form alloys, create van der Waals heterostructures, etc. provides further means to modulate their properties which have led to a number of interesting luminescence features and potential applications in lighting, imaging and sensing. As the map of 2D materials has expanded over the past few years, 2D organic and organic–inorganic hybrid materials, such as 2D polymers, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), and organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites, have also emerged with merits of low cost, chemical versatility and solution processibility. Importantly, the wide tunability of their functional properties is enabled by the rational design and controlled modification of their compositions and structures at the molecular level. Herein, we present a critical review of 2D light-emitting materials by categorizing them into three main groups, namely 2D inorganic light-emitting materials, 2D organic light-emitting materials, and 2D organic–inorganic hybrid light-emitting materials. Within each group, we will describe their preparation methods and discuss their structural and chemical properties. Significant emphasis will be placed on strategies that can tune their luminescence properties, and their potential applications in various fields. Finally, we will summarize by stating the current challenges and future opportunities in the development of 2D light-emitting materials.
## Introduction
The recently intensified research in two-dimensional (2D) materials, such as graphene and transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs), has been motivated by many of their phenomenal properties and the possibility to revolutionize the future electronic/ optoelectronic technologies.1–6 In particular, semiconducting 2D light-emitting materials like graphene derivatives,3 TMDs6 and phosphorene2 have demonstrated a number of attractive features such as tunable band structures,2,5–10 large exciton binding energies,11,12 strong light–matter interactions6,13 and high carrier mobility,6,7,9,14 not to mention the flexibility that makes these atomic thin crystals promising for the nextgeneration optoelectronic devices, including optical sensors, displays, lasers, and light-emitting diodes (LEDs).1–3,6 In contrast to the zero bandgap graphene, graphene derivatives such as chemically modified graphene, graphene quantum dots and graphene nanoribbons possess finite and tunable bandgaps for light-emitting purposes. The development in this research area has been reviewed in several recent publications.3,5,14 Single- to few-layer TMDs are superior to graphene derivatives and show direct and indirect bandgaps, respectively, over the visible to near-infrared range.5–9 Phosphorene, a very unique type of 2D material, possesses widely tunable direct bandgaps (0.3 and $2.0~\mathrm{eV}$ for bulk and monolayer, respectively) and in-plane anisotropy which make it distinctly different from graphene and TMDs.2,10 The light-emitting properties of these materials, including photoluminescence (PL),5,15 electroluminescence (EL),5,15 thermoluminescence $\mathrm{(TL)}^{16}$ and cathodoluminescence (CL),17 can be tuned by many factors, such as substrate, temperature, electrostatic doping, strain and defect.2,5,15 Moreover, the building of heterostructures of 2D materials by vertically stacking them or laterally stitching them together provides further opportunities to overcome the limitations of constituent layers and also generate novel properties from interactions/ coupling between them.18–20
Promising research on the aforementioned inorganic 2D materials further enriched the exploration of 2D organic and organic–inorganic hybrid light-emitting materials, such as organic small molecules, polymers, supramolecular polymers, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent-organic frameworks (COFs), organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites, and organic metal complexes.21–25 As newly emerged 2D materials, 2D organic and organic–inorganic hybrid light-emitting materials have exhibited controllable synthesis, easy assembly, flexible structures and excellent optical/photoelectric properties.26–28 They represent a unique class of nanomaterials that possess single- or few-atom thick sheet-like structures with the lateral size up to a few micrometers. Their 2D feature is unique and indispensable to access unprecedented chemical, physical, electronic, and optical properties over their 3D counterparts due to the electron confinement effect.29
In this review, we will firstly describe the structures and preparation methods of various semiconducting 2D inorganic light-emitting materials. Combined with the available theoretical predictions, a significant emphasis will be placed on their light-emitting properties and strategies that can tune these properties for various applications. Then, we review the recent advances in 2D organic and organic–inorganic hybrid light-emitting materials, starting with a discussion of their various synthetic methods with comments on their advantages and limitations. After introducing the characterization of their optical/optoelectronic properties, their potential use in light-emitting devices as well as in other electronic and optoelectronic applications will be reviewed. Finally, current challenges and opportunities in the development of 2D lightemitting materials will be stated.
## 2D inorganic light-emitting materials
## 1. Transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs)
Layered TMDs with the chemical formula of $\mathbf{MX}_{2}$ , where M represents a transition metal (e.g., Mo and W) and $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}$ is a chalcogen (e.g., S, Se and Te), are probably one class of the most investigated inorganic 2D materials. Every TMD monolayer consists of two chalcogen planes separated by a transition metal plane.1,9 Adjacent individual layers are held together to build the corresponding 3D form via van der Waals interactions.
