Due to the variation in metal–chalcogen coordination and/or stacking orders between layers, different crystal polytypes exist for TMDs.30,31 Taking MoS2 as an example, it has four polytypes, that is 2H, 1T, $\Omega^{\prime}$ and 3R (Fig. 1a).31 The polytype with trigonal prismatic coordination is referred to as 2H and its single layer form is referred to as 1H, while the octahedral coordination corresponds to the 1T phase. Along the $z$ -axis normal to the 2D plane, the atomic stacking sequence of the 2H phase is S–Mo–S (ABA) in the hexagonal symmetry, and that of the 1T phase is S–Mo– ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{S}}^{\prime}$ (ABA0) in the tetragonal symmetry. The $\mathbf{1T^{\prime}}$ phase, which is also known as the disordered 1T phase, has a similar octahedral coordination to the 1T phase. However, the $\Omega^{\prime}$ phase includes a superstructure in every layer (e.g. zigzag chain $(2a\times a)$ , trimerization $\left({\sqrt{3a}}\times{\sqrt{3a}}\right)$ and tetramerization $(2a\times2a),$ .1,31 The 3R phase has three layers per repeat unit in the rhombohedral symmetry with trigonal prismatic coordination. The dominant phase of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ is 2H due to its thermodynamic preference, and other phases will transform to 2H upon heating.32,33
